{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016nX9KHHgA3QQvamD5kJefu","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Now Phase 8: Clean up mcp_server.py. The plan says to remove `_start_opencode_serve()` since the lifecycle manager handles it now. However, `_start_opencode_serve` is used in the MCP server's `app_lifespan` for the teams functionality — it auto-starts `opencode serve` for the teams tools. The service_lifecycle in app.py handles it for the dashboard/chat. These are separate contexts.\n\nLooking at the code more carefully, the MCP server's `_start_opencode_serve()` is specifically for the teams tools' opencode integration. The lifecycle manager in `app.py` is for the architecture chat feature. Since the teams tools are commented out, the `_start_opencode_serve` is still used but only for the lifespan state's `opencode_server_url`. This is needed for the teams tools when they're re-enabled.\n\nI'll consolidate by making `mcp_server.py` delegate to the lifecycle manager's `opencode_config()` instead of its own `_start_opencode_serve()`. But since the lifecycle manager is async and the MCP lifespan already has its own pattern, and since the teams tools are commented out, I'll take a cleaner approach: remove `_start_opencode_serve()` and replace the auto-start block to use the lifecycle manager's singleton (which is already started by the dashboard thread's app.py lifespan).\n\nLet me look more carefully at how the MCP server interacts with the dashboard:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":357,"cache_read_input_tokens":128721,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":357},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"41eb5763-1813-40b4-994f-a8833bfca10e","uuid":"d6ecc08e-42d3-49a6-a470-83c4255b8393"}
